# Backend (NestJS) configuration.
#
# Copy this file to .env and adjust as needed:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored and must never be committed. Only this template is.
# Values are read via ConfigService (see src/main.ts); anything set in your
# shell environment overrides what is written here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployed environments (RUN-60)
#
# The same variable names are set in the HOST's env config, never in a
# committed file (AC3). On Render they come from render.yaml: DATABASE_URL is
# wired straight from the managed database, while JWT_SECRET, FRONTEND_URL and
# ROUTING_API_KEY are declared "sync: false", which makes Render prompt for
# them and keeps them out of git entirely. Each variable below notes what its
# deployed value looks like.
#
# A wrong value fails the boot on purpose (src/config/env.validation.ts), with
# every problem listed at once, so a broken first deploy explains itself in the
# service logs instead of dying later on the first request that needs it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Port the API listens on. The frontend expects 3000 by default.
# DEPLOYED: do not set it. The host injects PORT and health-checks that exact
# port; main.ts reads it through ConfigService and falls back to 3000.
PORT=3000

# Origin allowed by CORS.
# DEPLOYED (AC3): the frontend's real origin, scheme + host, NO trailing slash,
# e.g. https://run-log-app.vercel.app
#
# Be clear about what this is and is not. The app's own browser traffic never
# uses CORS: the browser calls its own origin and Next rewrites /api/* to this
# backend server-side. So a wrong value here does NOT break the deployed app,
# and no amount of clicking around will reveal it. It is the allow-list for a
# genuinely cross-origin caller, which today means a hand-written fetch or a
# future client that skips the rewrite. Set it correctly anyway - a stale
# allow-list is a trap for whoever adds such a caller later.
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:4200

# PostgreSQL connection string (RUN-46). Create the empty database once
# (CREATE DATABASE runlog;), fill in your local credentials, then run
# `npx prisma migrate dev` to build the schema. Read by ConfigService at
# runtime (PrismaService) and by the Prisma CLI via prisma.config.ts.
#
# DEPLOYED: a managed database's URL, and SSL is where this bites.
#   - Render, service and database in the same region: use the INTERNAL URL
#     (render.yaml wires it automatically). Private networking, so no sslmode
#     parameter is needed.
#   - Any connection from outside the host - your laptop, CI, a GUI client, or
#     a managed database from another provider such as Neon - is over the public
#     internet and needs TLS:
#         postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DB?sslmode=require
#     Leaving sslmode off there fails with an SSL handshake error, not a
#     permission error, which is the clue to look for.
# Either form starts with postgresql://, which is what validateEnv checks.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:<password>@localhost:5432/runlog"

# Secret used to sign JWTs (RUN-56). Required at boot, minimum 32 characters.
# Generate your own once and keep it out of git:
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# DEPLOYED (AC3): a SECRET. Generate a fresh one for the deployed environment
# rather than reusing a local value, and paste it into the host's env config
# only. Changing it invalidates every issued token, signing everyone out.
JWT_SECRET="<generate-a-random-secret-at-least-32-chars>"

# --- Route planning (RUN-53), OPTIONAL ---------------------------------------
# Unlike the two above, these are not required to boot. Leave them out and the
# whole app still runs; only POST /api/routes/plan answers 503 with
# code "ROUTING_NOT_CONFIGURED", which the Add run modal shows while still
# letting you save the run by hand.
#
# openrouteservice API key, for the foot-walking profile. The public OSRM demo
# server was the other spike candidate and was rejected: it answers with car
# results for every profile, including the foot one (see routes.service.ts).
# Free key, no card, takes about a minute: https://openrouteservice.org/dev/
# It stays server-side - never copy it into the frontend or give it a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
# DEPLOYED: a SECRET, and worth setting before demoing route maps (RUN-54 is
# the UI that calls this endpoint).
# ROUTING_API_KEY="<your-openrouteservice-api-key>"

# Only set this to point at a self-hosted openrouteservice instance. Omitted,
# the hosted API (https://api.openrouteservice.org) is used.
# ROUTING_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080/ors
